<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Abu Yusuf</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 729–798</span> · <a href="/centuries/8th-century">8th century</a> · Also known as Yaʿqūb ibn Ibrāhīm al-Anṣārī, Abū Yūsuf</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Abu Yusuf, born Ya'qub ibn Ibrahim al-Ansari, was a Muslim jurist of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kufa">Kufa</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a> and one of the foremost authorities of the early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanafi-school">Hanafi school</a> of Islamic law. The leading student of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abu-hanifa">Abu Hanifa</a>, he also studied under Malik ibn Anas in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medina">Medina</a>. The Abbasid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harun-al-rashid">Harun al-Rashid</a> made him Grand <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qadi">Qadi</a> (qadi al-qudat), the first time the title had been conferred in Islamic history, with the power to appoint other judges, and through this office Hanafi law spread in the courts of the state. His Kitab al-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kharaj">Kharaj</a>, written at the caliph's request, is one of the earliest systematic treatises on taxation and public finance in Islamic law.</p></div>
